      "summary": "Samsung's HBM4 Yield Hits 80% in Race to Challenge SK Hynix's AI Memory Lead Startup Fortune",
      "body": "Samsung's reported HBM4 yield jump to about 80% gives it a real shot at clawing back ground in AI memory, but SK Hynix still holds the customer relationships and order share that matter most.\nSamsung has reached the number it needed. Seoul Economic Daily reported on August 10 that Samsung Electronics' HBM4 yield has come close to 80%, up from below 60% when mass production started in February. ZDNet Korea also cited the report, and SamMobile later said the company had crossed the same milestone. That's fast. For a chip as hard to build as high-bandwidth memory, yield isn't an accounting detail. Yield is money.\nThe phrase you keep seeing around this story is golden yield, the point where a manufacturing process becomes stable enough to make real volume profitable. Seoul Economic Daily said Samsung had expected to hit that level near the end of 2026. Getting there in August changes the conversation because HBM4 is the memory tied to Nvidia's Vera Rubin AI platform and AMD's next MI400 ge",
